This is the good life.

20
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:11,860
We have it good, don't you think?

21
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:13,280
Yeah, of course.

22
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,660
Although, don't you ever wonder what could have been?

23
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:17,780
What do you mean?

24
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:23,610
Well, sometimes I think of how things could have turned out if I had done things a little differently.

25
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:24,650
For example?

26
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:31,010
Like, for example, if I hadn't studied architecture, I would have become an artist like I wanted to.

27
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:32,080
I see.

28
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:37,340
Yeah, now that I think of it, I wouldn't have gotten married if I hadn't moved to this town and met Sally.

29
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:40,580
You see, everything happens for a reason.

30
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,020
We wouldn't even have met if I hadn't been in that car accident ten years ago.

31
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:46,940
Well, I have no regrets.

32
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:48,320
I'll drink to that.

33
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,380
All right, so two guys, you know, talking about life.

34
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:05,900
I imagine these guys as being, you know, middle age or a little bit older, sitting on a porch, drinking a beer, talking about what could have been.

35
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:07,000
Yeah, exactly.

36
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:11,200
They're kind of thinking about the past and the decisions they have made.

37
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:12,880
Yeah, exactly.

38
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:19,020
All right, so before we start in the grammar, why don't we take a look at a couple of different phrases on Fluency Builder.

39
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:21,210
Fluency Builder.

40
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,370
Well, Marco, this first phrase is a phrase you'll hear very often in English.

41
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:33,670
It's a phrase that even has philosophical roots, something we talked a lot about when we were studying Socrates back in school.

42
00:02:33,670 --> 00:02:35,510
But this is the good life.

43
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:36,690
The good life.

44
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:39,270
We have the good life or I have a good life.

45
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,700
I have a good life is a very common phrase, but here the good life is an idiom.

46
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:46,560
So I'm living the good life.

47
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,680
It means, you know, I have no complaints about my life.

48
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:52,000
Right, so pretty much you have all your necessities covered.

49
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:57,450
And maybe you have a good, a nice family and you have a good job and you're just happy.

50
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:58,750
So this is the good life.

51
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:00,090
Very good.

52
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:02,090
So you have the good life.

53
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:10,050
Now moving on to another phrase that's kind of also philosophical, you know, it says everything happens for a reason.

54
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:11,750
All right.

55
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:15,170
So this phrase is, well, it's also pretty common.

56
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:18,570
People say this when bad things happen, but they also say this when good things happen.

57
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:19,840
Yeah.

58
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,870
So basically you're just saying that it's destiny.

59
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:25,810
If this bad thing happened to you, there is a reason for it.

60
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:30,770
Maybe you don't see it now, maybe not tomorrow, but maybe later you'll see why.

61
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:33,810
This often happens when people lose their jobs.

62
00:03:34,450 --> 00:03:36,270
You say, well, you know, everything happens for a reason.

63
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:42,320
Maybe losing this job will allow you to find a better job.

64
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:43,880
Or maybe go back to school and study.

65
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,700
Or maybe meet the man or woman of your dreams.

66
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:47,760
Right.

67
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:49,740
So everything happens for a reason.

68
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,120
Very good.

69
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,280
And then the guy agrees and says, well, I'll drink to that.

70
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:54,060
Okay.

71
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:59,200
So this is a way for people speaking English to say, listen, I agree with you.

72
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:02,560
I like that idea and I will, cheers.

73
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:03,300
Cheers.

74
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:04,720
Right.

75
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:05,720
Right.

76
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:13,560
So this means we're going to touch our glasses or touch our beer bottles and we're going to, you know, think about how this is a, it's a nice thought, you know, everything happens for a reason is positive.

77
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:15,570
It's a way to say, listen, good things can happen from this.

78
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:18,400
So he's saying, yes, I agree.

79
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:18,910
Cheers.

80
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:19,690
I'll drink to that.

81
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:20,570
I'll drink to that.

82
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,400
All right.

83
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:22,970
Well, pretty interesting phrases.

84
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:27,440
You know, they're very common, I guess, like colloquial, right?

85
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,620
So you can use them in everyday conversation with a friend.

86
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:30,800
You can.

87
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:38,980
And, you know, oftentimes we say, for example, something really positive, like, I'm really glad I have such great friends.

88
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,860
And then everyone says, oh, I'll drink to that.

89
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:41,440
Right.

90
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:44,920
So this is something you'll hear when you're out with friends or with family.

91
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:50,640
And they may sound funny at first, but you will recognize them when you're speaking to people who speak English.

92
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:51,340
All right.

93
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:52,880
So, all right.

94
00:04:52,880 --> 00:05:01,220
So let's listen to our dialogue again and then we'll come back and explain the grammar.

95
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:02,560
This is the good life.

96
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:04,740
We have it good, don't you think?

97
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:06,900
Yeah, of course.

98
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:11,660
Although don't you ever wonder what could have been?

99
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:13,480
What do you mean?

100
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:21,160
Well, sometimes I think of how things could have turned out if I had done things a little differently.

101
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:22,560
For example?

102
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:30,650
Like, for example, if I hadn't studied architecture, I would have become an artist like I wanted to.

103
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:31,890
I see.

104
00:05:32,490 --> 00:05:32,870
Yeah.

105
00:05:33,210 --> 00:05:38,150
Now that I think of it, I wouldn't have gotten married if I hadn't moved to this town and met Sally.

106
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:42,490
You see, everything happens for a reason.

107
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:47,810
We wouldn't even have met if I hadn't been in that car accident ten years ago.

108
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:50,410
Well, I have no regrets.

109
00:05:51,110 --> 00:05:52,290
I'll drink to that.

110
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:56,280
Beep, beep, beep.

111
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:57,640
All right.

112
00:05:57,640 --> 00:05:59,980
So now it's time for grammar breakdown.

113
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:02,590
Grammar breakdown.

114
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:10,000
So in grammar breakdown, we're going to look at the two main parts of these phrases that allow us to talk about things that didn't happen.

115
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:16,160
So we're talking about looking back at our life and seeing what decisions and what actions we made.

116
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:19,960
And in this case, we have a great sentence here.

117
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:25,560
If I hadn't studied architecture, I would have become an artist.

118
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,080
All right.

119
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:29,800
So let's look at the first part before the comma.

120
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:33,580
If I hadn't studied architecture, so now we have the conditional if.

121
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:35,500
So Marco, big question.

122
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:36,660
Did he study architecture?

123
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:37,580
He did.

124
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:39,280
He did.

125
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:42,140
So if I hadn't studied architecture, it means I did study architecture.

126
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:51,230
But if I had not studied it, okay, so this is if I could change the past, then what would I have done?

127
00:06:51,610 --> 00:06:53,630
I would have become an artist.

128
00:06:54,130 --> 00:06:54,270
Okay.

129
00:06:54,470 --> 00:06:57,970
So he wanted to be an artist, but he didn't become an artist.

130
00:06:58,230 --> 00:06:59,770
He studied architecture instead.

131
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:00,530
Right.

132
00:07:00,970 --> 00:07:01,290
Okay.

133
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:03,280
Very good.

134
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:04,280
You can see the two parts here.

135
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:07,040
We have one, the condition, if I hadn't done this thing.

136
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:10,950
And then afterwards, we have the result, then I would have become an artist.

137
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,090
So this is the hypothetical.

138
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:12,930
Very good.

139
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:21,710
And when we see the condition, you see the structure of the sentence is had plus verb in past participle.

140
00:07:21,920 --> 00:07:27,560
So if I hadn't studied architecture, and this is called the past perfect, right?

141
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:36,160
And then the result, as you said, would have become, this is the present perfect, have or has plus verb in past participle.

142
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:37,110
Let's look at another example.

143
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:43,210
I wouldn't have gotten married if I hadn't moved to this town.

144
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:44,790
Okay.

145
00:07:44,790 --> 00:07:47,810
So we've got the same idea here, but we've got it flipped.

146
00:07:48,470 --> 00:07:50,630
We've got these two parts in different places.

147
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:58,480
And so he's pretty much saying, if I hadn't moved to this town, then I wouldn't have gotten married.

148
00:07:58,480 --> 00:07:59,010
But what did happen?

149
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:01,530
He did move to this town and he did get married.

150
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:03,040
Exactly.

151
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:05,670
So then, yeah, like you say, he changed the condition and you can do this.

152
00:08:05,820 --> 00:08:08,310
You can put the condition in the beginning or at the end.

153
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:11,040
And again, the structure is the same.

154
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:13,150
I would have gotten married, right?

155
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:20,320
Have gotten, this is present perfect, have or has, depending on the subject, plus the verb in past participle.

156
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:27,080
And then the past perfect or the condition, had plus verb in past participle, hadn't moved to this town.

157
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:28,180
All right.

158
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:29,000
That's a lot of grammar.

159
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:33,740
It's a lot of grammar, but it's very easy once you just start using it.

160
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:36,440
Don't think too much about, okay, what do I do here?

161
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:42,960
Just, you know, if you get the rhythm and you start using it in the same way, then it kind of comes out naturally.

162
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,820
And if you make a little mistake, there's not a big deal because even we do it, you know?

163
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:46,260
Exactly.

164
00:08:46,580 --> 00:08:48,800
Well, let's try a couple of examples off the top of our heads.

165
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:56,090
First of all, if I hadn't moved to China, I wouldn't have learned Chinese.

166
00:08:56,630 --> 00:08:57,030
Very good.

167
00:08:57,350 --> 00:08:59,830
So you did move to China and you did learn Chinese.

168
00:08:59,870 --> 00:09:00,430
Exactly.

169
00:09:00,950 --> 00:09:01,590
So your turn.

170
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:03,840
All right.

171
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:09,470
If my parents hadn't spoken Spanish to me when I was a kid, I wouldn't have become fluent in Spanish.

172
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:10,620
Okay.

173
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:14,740
So your parents did speak Spanish to you and you did become fluent in Spanish.

174
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:15,760
So not bad.

175
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:16,200
Right.

176
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:28,040
So maybe you can use it not only to think about things in the past that you can't change anymore, but just also, you know, kind of remember or say, you know, oh, if this hadn't happened, the result.

177
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:34,030
Or if I hadn't opened my big mouth, my best friend wouldn't have gotten so angry at me.

178
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:35,640
Exactly.

179
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:39,760
So I said something stupid and my friend did get angry and now I feel bad about it.

180
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:40,760
It happens.

181
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:41,760
All right.

182
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:42,760
Very good.

183
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:43,760
So I hope it's clear.

184
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:46,640
It does sound a little bit complicated, but this is what our website is for, right?

185
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:51,530
So if you have questions or doubts or you want to try and plant your own ideas, you can come to the website and do it.

186
00:09:52,080 --> 00:10:00,410
Want to make some of your own sentences and practice some of these new things, then come to our website, post some comments and we are happy to correct and add our own commentary on those.

187
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:02,160
Exactly.

188
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:04,490
So before we go, let's listen to this dialogue one last time.

189
